      "content": "There is no consensus among legal scholars on which approach is \"correct,\" as the debate over \"substantive due process\" is one of the most contentious issues in American constitutional law today.\nWhile \"textualism\" is often favored by the legal right as a neutral method of strictly following the text, \"living tree\" or \"unenumerated rights\" arguments are prevalent on the left and center right as necessary to protect evolving civil liberties.\nUltimately, the \"correct\" approach is a matter of political and ideological preference rather than an established legal fact, and the best way to teach this is to present the arguments for both sides side by side without declaring one side the \"winner.\"",
